WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 219: Germany: Otto von Bismarck, 'Blood and Iron', and the Unification of Germany

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:13.760
[Rebecca] Today we witnessed the birth of modern Germany under one of the most formidable statesmen in world history, Otto von Bismarck.

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:20.560
[Rebecca] Armed with ruthless pragmatic genius and diplomatic mastery, the Iron Chancellor remade the map of Europe.

5
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Bismarck, Blood and Iron, and the unification of Germany.

6
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:42.000
[George] Born in April 1815, the fateful year of Napoleon's final defeat at Waterloo, Otto Eduard Leopold von Bismarck was the son of an ancient Prussian junker aristocratic family in Brandenburg.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:59.040
[George] As a student at the University of Gurtingen, the towering, imposing youth earned a reputation for wild carousing, heavy beer drinking, and fierce aggression, fighting 26 duels with sabers without suffering a single wound except a scar on his cheek from a shattered blade.

8
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:13.760
[George] Despising the dull routine of bureaucratic paperwork, young Bismarck spent years managing his rural family estates in Pomerania, known by his neighbors as the mad junker for shooting holes through the ceilings of his friends' bedrooms to wake them up.

9
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:26.720
[George] Yet beneath this Bohemian bravado lay an extraordinary, towering intellect, a photographic memory, fluency in five languages, and a profound philosophical understanding of European diplomacy.

10
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:33.760
[George] A spiritual conversion through his devout Lutheran wife, Johanna von Puttkamer, grounded his temperament.

11
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:47.680
[George] Entering Prussian politics during the revolutionary upheavals of 1848, Bismarck emerged as an arch-conservative defender of the Hohenzollern crown, arguing that power, not parliamentary debates, governed the world.

12
00:01:47.680 --> 00:02:01.240
[George] In his wild youth at the universities of Gurtingen and Berlin, Otto von Bismarck fought 25 duels with broadswords, drank heavily, and horrified conservative neighbors as the reckless mad junker of Schurnhausen.

13
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:13.640
[George] Yet beneath his unruly exterior lay an intellect of razor-sharp brilliance, an encyclopedic grasp of European diplomacy, and an unshakable loyalty to the conservative monarchy of the Hohenzollerns.

14
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.480
[George] In 1862, the Kingdom of Prussia faced an existential constitutional deadlock.

15
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:30.200
[George] The Liberal-dominated Prussian parliament refused to approve tax funds requested by King Wilhelm I and War Minister Albrecht von Ruhn to expand and reorganize the Prussian army.

16
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:35.640
[George] On the verge of abdication, the king appointed Bismarck as minister president of Prussia.

17
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.200
[George] Bismarck immediately took charge with fearless, authoritarian audacity.

18
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:47.240
[George] He bypassed the parliament, governed without an approved budget, and collected military taxes by royal decree.

19
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:56.600
[George] On September 30, 1862, Bismarck addressed the Parliamentary Budget Committee, delivering words that would define his historical legacy.

20
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.280
[George] The position of Prussia in Germany will not be determined by its liberalism but by its power.

21
00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:09.400
[George] Prussia must concentrate and maintain its power for the favorable moment, which has already slipped by several times.

22
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:20.040
[George] Not through speeches and majority decisions are the great questions of the day decided, that was the great mistake of 1848 and 1849, but by iron and blood.

23
00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.320
[George] Bismarck recognized that German unification could never be achieved through liberal debates.

24
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.920
[George] But only under conservative Prussian military leadership.

25
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:42.360
[George] In September 1862, with Prussia paralyzed by a bitter constitutional showdown between King Wilhelm I and the Liberal-dominated parliament over military budgets, the king appointed Bismarck as minister-president.

26
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.680
[George] Facing the parliamentary budget committee, Bismarck delivered his immortal declaration.

27
00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.720
[George] The great questions of the time will not be resolved by speeches and majority decisions.

28
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.400
[George] That was the great mistake of 1848 and 1849, but by iron and blood.

29
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:12.880
[George] Bismarck's statecraft was defined by real politics, the calculated pursuit of national self-interest based on realistic, practical assessments of power and circumstances, free from moral sentimentality or ideological dogma.

30
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:21.440
[George] Bismarck viewed diplomacy as the art of the possible, famously remarking: Politics is not an exact science, but an art.

31
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.240
[George] A statesman must wait until he hears the footsteps of God marching through history.

32
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.600
[George] And then leap forward and catch on to his coattails.

33
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:41.200
[George] His overarching strategic goal was to unite the German states under the Prussian monarchy while expelling Prussia's traditional Catholic rival, the Austrian Habsburg Empire, from German affairs.

34
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:52.000
[George] In 1864, Bismarck orchestrated his first masterstroke, allying with Austria to defeat Denmark in a brief war over the border duchies of Schleswig and Holstein.

35
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:04.480
[George] By jointly administering the captured territories, Bismarck deliberately engineered administrative frictions with Vienna, laying the trap for the decisive confrontation that would settle the leadership of the German world.

36
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:16.880
[George] Bismarck became the undisputed master of Realpolitik, the pragmatic pursuit of national self-interest untethered to moralizing dogma, personal sentiment, or ideological orthodoxy.

37
00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:32.760
[George] Viewing the diplomatic landscape like a grand chessboard, Bismarck skillfully exploited the rivalries of Austria, France, Russia, and Britain, isolating each opponent in turn while keeping multiple strategic options open until the decisive moment to strike arrived.

38
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:41.960
[George] In the summer of 1866, Bismarck sprang his trap, provoking the Austro-Prussian War, known to history as the Seven Weeks' War.

39
00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:50.520
[George] Most European observers predicted an Austrian victory, assuming the Habsburg Empire's larger population and military prestige would prevail.

40
00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:57.960
[George] Instead, the world was stunned by the Prussian military machine organized by Chief of Staff Helmuth von Moltke.

41
00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:12.600
[George] Mobilizing troops with unprecedented speed along state-oned railway lines, and equipping infantry with the breechloading needle gun that fired three times faster than Austrian muzzle loaders, the Prussian army outmaneuvered the Austrians.

42
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:21.080
[George] On July 3, 1866, at the climactic Battle of Kernigretz in Bohemia, Moltke shattered the Austrian army.

43
00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:26.200
[George] At this triumphant moment, Bismarck revealed his supreme diplomatic genius.

44
00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:33.400
[George] King Wilhelm and his generals wanted to march into Vienna in a grand triumphal parade and annex Austrian territory.

45
00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:45.480
[George] Bismarck threatened suicide and wept in screaming arguments to stop them, insisting that Austria must not be permanently alienated, but treated with leniency so it would remain a future ally.

46
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:53.640
[George] Austria lost no territory, but agreed to withdraw from German affairs, leaving Prussia to form the North German Confederation.

47
00:06:53.640 --> 00:07:08.120
[George] In the summer of 1866, Bismarck provoked Austria into the Seven Weeks War, unleashing the modernized Prussian army equipped with breechloading needle guns and directed by General Helmuth von Molt's telegraphic general staff.

48
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:21.440
[George] At the climactic Battle of Sadoa, Prussian troops crushed the Austrian army, expelling the Habsburgs from German affairs and uniting all lands north of the main river under Prussian leadership in the North German Confederation.

49
00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:31.520
[George] To complete German unification, Bismarck needed to bring the reluctant, Catholic South German kingdoms, Bavaria, Wuatemberg, and Baden, into the fold.

50
00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:42.560
[George] Bismarck recognized that only a common, existential threat could bridge the deep cultural divide between North and South, a war with France under Emperor Napoleon III.

51
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:59.680
[George] In July 1870, when a Spanish succession crisis provoked French diplomatic protests, French ambassador Count Benedetti approached King Wilhelm at the Spa Resort of Bad M's, politely requesting a royal guarantee that a Prussian prince would never seek the Spanish crown.

52
00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.800
[George] Wilhelm sent a detailed telegram to Bismarck summarizing the polite interview.

53
00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.280
[George] Dining with Moltk and Rune, Bismarck saw his historic opportunity.

54
00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:21.600
[George] Taking a red pencil, Bismarck ruthlessly edited the M's dispatch, deleting words without altering facts, so that the polite exchange read as an insolent French demand and an abrupt royal insult.

55
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.840
[George] Bismarck leaked the edited telegram to the press on Bastille Day.

56
00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:40.560
[George] The French public exploded in patriotic fury, and on July 19, 1870, Napoleon III foolishly declared war on Prussia, falling directly into Bismarck's trap as the South German states rallied to Prussia's defense.

57
00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.680
[George] To draw the reluctant Catholic states of southern Germany into a unified nation.

58
00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:58.080
[George] Bismarck engineered a confrontation with France by cleverly editing the M's dispatch, a royal telegram from King Wilhelm, to make it appear that the Prussian king and the French ambassador had insulted each other.

59
00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:08.600
[George] The calculated provocation sparked explosive nationalist outrage in Paris, prompting Emperor Napoleon III to declare war, exactly as Bismarck had planned.

60
00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:12.040
[George] The Franco-Prussian War was a lightning military route.

61
00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:26.200
[George] Molk's armies, utilizing rail logistics and Krupp steel artillery, trapped the French army at Sedan on September 1, 1870, capturing Napoleon III himself along with 100,000 French soldiers.

62
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:31.320
[George] Advancing on Paris, the German armies laid a suffocating four-month siege.

63
00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:44.120
[George] On January 18, 1871, exactly 170 years to the day since the first King of Prussia had been crowned, Bismarck staged the supreme, symbolic triumph of German history.

64
00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:58.360
[George] Inside the historic Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles, surrounded by hundreds of German princes, generals and standard bearers, King Wilhelm I was proclaimed Kaiser Wilhelm I, Emperor of a United German Empire.

65
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:08.360
[George] The centuries-old fragmentation of Germany was erased in a single ceremony, creating an economic, industrial and military colossus in the center of Europe.

66
00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:19.480
[George] But Bismarck insisted on annexing the French provinces of Alsace and Lorraine, sowing an unquenchable seed of French bitterness that would explode 43 years later in World War I.

67
00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:23.240
[George] The Franco-Prussian War ended in total French catastrophe.

68
00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:38.120
[George] Napoleon III and his entire army surrendered at Sedan, Paris was besieged, and on January 18, 1871, in the opulent Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles, King Wilhelm I was proclaimed German Emperor.

69
00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:49.600
[George] Bismarck had achieved the long cherished dream of German national unification, forging a unified Reich that instantly became the preeminent industrial and military powerhouse of continental Europe.

70
00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:59.520
[George] As Chancellor of the new German Empire from 1871 to 1890, Bismarck surprised Europe by becoming a passionate champion of continental peace.

71
00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:23.680
[George] Declaring that Germany was a satiated nation with no further territorial ambitions, Bismarck constructed an intricate, masterly web of overlapping diplomatic alliances, the Three Emperors' League, the Triple Alliance with Austria and Italy, and the secret reinsurance treaty with Russia, designed to keep France diplomatically isolated and prevent Germany from ever fighting a two-front war.

72
00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:42.960
[George] At home, to combat the rising socialist movement, Bismarck pioneered the world's very first modern social welfare state, introducing health insurance in 1883, accident insurance in 1884, and old-age pensions in 1889, proving that conservative monarchies could protect workers.

73
00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.000
[George] In 1888, the youthful, arrogant and bellicose Kaiser Wilhelm I.

74
00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.840
[George] ascended the throne, clashing with the aging Chancellor.

75
00:11:51.840 --> 00:12:00.960
[George] In March 1890, Wilhelm forced Bismarck to resign, immortalized in Punch magazine's famous cartoon, Dropping the Pilot.

76
00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:07.840
[George] Living in bitter retirement until his death in 1898, Bismarck issued a chilling, prophetic warning.

77
00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:13.040
[George] One day the great European war will come out of some damned foolish thing in the Balkans.

78
00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.840
[George] Sixteen years later, the pilot's ship of state steered straight into the abyss.

79
00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:36.920
[George] As Imperial Chancellor for the next 19 years, Bismarck maintained European peace through an intricate, balancing web of secret defensive alliances, while simultaneously instituting the world's first modern welfare state with accident, sickness, and old age pensions to undercut the socialist movement.

80
00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:40.440
[George] In 1890, the arrogant young Kaiser Wilhelm I.

81
00:12:51.720 --> 00:13:01.880
[Rebecca] Otto von Bismarck forged a united German Empire through blood and iron, leaving behind a modern superpower and an intricate European balance of power.

82
00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:08.280
[Rebecca] South of the Alps, the ancient peninsula of Italy was undergoing an equally dramatic rebirth.

83
00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:13.880
[Rebecca] Next week we cross into Italy with episode 220, the Italian Renaissance.

84
00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:25.080
[Rebecca] Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo, and the Medici of Florence, the rebirth of art, the sealing of the Sistine Chapel, the Mona Lisa, and the Golden Court of Lorenzo the Magnificent.

85
00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:36.520
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

